# Hollow Metal–Organic‐Framework Micro/Nanostructures and their Derivatives: Emerging Multifunctional Materials

## abstract
Hollow metal-organic framework (MOF) micro/nanostructures and their derivatives are attracting a great amount of research interest in recent years because their hierarchical porous structures not only provide abundant, easily accessed metal sites but also endow 3D channels for rapid mass transport. As a result, they demonstrate significant advantages in many applications including catalysis, gas sensors, batteries, supercapacitors, and so on. Nevertheless, studies on hollow MOFs and their derivatives are still at the beginning of this field, and the relationship between their structures and application performances is not yet reviewed comprehensively. Herein, the synthetic strategies and practical applications of hollow micro/nanostructured MOFs and their derivatives are summarized, and their corresponding prospects are also discussed.

Hollow metal–organic framework (MOF) micro/nanostructures and their derivatives are attracting a great amount of research interest in recent years because their hierarchical porous structures not only provide abundant, easily accessed metal sites but also endow 3D channels for rapid mass transport. As a result, they demonstrate significant advantages in many applications including catalysis, gas sensors, batteries, supercapacitors, and so on. Nevertheless, studies on hollow MOFs and their derivatives are still at the beginning of this field, and the relationship between their structures and application performances is not yet reviewed comprehensively. Herein, the synthetic strategies and practical applications of hollow micro/nanostructured MOFs and their derivatives are summarized, and their corresponding prospects are also discussed.
## 1. Introduction
Hollow micro/nanostructures are a major type of advanced materials that possess unique features, including low density, functional walls, and accommodated cavity. They have been widely adopted as spatially confined reactors for various applications.[1] Meanwhile, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed by coordination interactions between metal ions and organic ligands are appealing materials because of their porous crystalline structure, rich composition, and easy preparation,[2] which endow them with significant properties in gas storage/separation,[3] catalysis,[4] and theragnosis.[5] Since hollow micro/nanostructures usually refer to materials with a cavity ranging from several nanometers to several micro­meters, and MOFs contain regular pores with sizes of less than two nanometers, hollow MOF micro/ nanostructures are characteristic hierarchical pore structures. Such hollow MOF structures will not only inherit the merits of MOFs, like their rich active sites, but also have additional advantages from the hollow structure, such as facile mass transport and exchange.[6] On the other hand, it is generally believed that most MOFs are of low chemical stability and bad electrochemical conductivity, which have hindered the application of hollow MOF micro/nanostructures in electrochemical fields such as electrocatalysis, lithium-ion batteries (LIBs), supercapacitors, and others. To solve these problems, various MOF-derived hollow structures have been designed, fabricated, and employed.[7]
With regard to the construction of hollow MOF micro/nanostructures, various methods have been developed, and we summarize them into three main synthetic strategies according to the different processes of the template construction and removal. They are named the exterior-template method, the self-template method, and the two-phase interface method (Figure  1). The exterior-template method uses a sacrificial template to build hollow-structured MOFs, which involves multiple-step procedures, including template fabrication, MOF growth, and template removal. This method performs well in simple design but with a complex process. Compared to the exterior-template method, there is no predesigned template at the beginning of the self-template method. The intermediate products not only act as template materials but also as precursors to form hollow-structured MOFs, which is greatly affected by the Kirkendall effect[8] or the Ostwald ripening process.[9] The final hollow MOF structures are thus determined by the formation of the intermediate products. In addition, the interface of two different phases is also utilized as a template and the MOF grows at this interface, which is classified as “twophase interface” synthesis. This method is mainly based on the difference in surface energy at the two-phase interface such as the gas–liquid, liquid—liquid, or liquid–solid interfaces.[10]
In contrast to hollow MOF micro/nanostructures, the synthetic strategy for MOF-derived hollow products aims at the bond breaking of the MOF structures. Two main routes including the pyrolysis method and the solid–liquid reaction are generally adopted (Figure 1). The pyrolysis method is a thermaltreatment process under different gas atmospheres. According to the atmosphere conditions, it can be further categorized into self-pyrolysis in an inert atmosphere and a solid–gas reaction with reactive gases/vapors. The solid–liquid reaction involves chemical reactions in solutions. The pyrolysis method and solid–liquid reaction are comprehensively employed in producing various hollow MOF derivatives including carbon matrixes, metal oxides, metal hydroxides, metal sulfides, metal phosphides, metal selenides, and so on.
It deserves to be pointed out that there have been several excellent reviews summarizing state-of-the art development of hollow micro/nanostructures,[6a,11] but neither focuses on the topic of hollow MOFs and their derivatives. Here, we first summarize the synthetic strategies of hollow MOFs and their derivatives. Afterward, we discuss their morphological and compositional effects on the applications in catalysis,[12] gas sensors,[13] batteries,[14] supercapacitors,[15] electrocatalysis,[16] and others.[17] Armed with discussions with several representative examples, the design and preparation strategies of hollow MOFs and their derivatives are clarified. To close, an outlook on hollow MOF micro/nanostructures and their derivatives is provided.
## 2. Preparation of Hollow MOF Micro/ Nanostructures and Their Derivatives
## 2.1. Hollow MOF Micro/Nanostructures
Different from conventional MOF nanoparticles, constructing hollow MOF micro/nanostructures demands more science and technology. In this section, we summarize the three main methods according to how the templates are built and removed (Figure 1). Among the different synthetic strategies, the exterior-template method is universal for the fabrication of various hollow MOF structures, while the self-template and two-phase interface methods are more convenient and suitable for scaled-up production.
## 2.1.1. Exterior-Template Method
The exterior-template method is a direct approach to construct hollow-structured MOFs with a template material, and commonly consists of three processes: 1) synthesis of the template, 2) MOF shell cladding, and 3) template removal. This method easily realizes geometric control over the hollow structures, such as shell thickness, interior space, pore order, etc. It is noteworthy that there is a competition between the homogeneous self-nucleation of MOFs in solution and the heterogeneous shell growth on the template surface during the whole formation process of hollow MOFs. Therefore, suitable surface modification on the template is necessary because it gives rise to an enhanced interaction between the MOF precursor and the template surface during the synthesis process.
Early in 2012, Oh and co-workers presented the first exteriortemplate method to fabricate hollow-structured ZIF-8 (ZIF: zeolitic imidazolate framework) by using carboxylate-terminated polystyrene (PS) spheres as the template.[18] As shown in Figure 2a, carboxylate-terminated PS spheres were synthesized with a uniform diameter. Subsequent addition of the PS spheres
