Besides hollow MOF structures with a single inner cavity, multicavity hollow MOFs have been designed and constructed by the exterior-template method. Su and co-workers reported multicavity hollow Au NPs@ZIF-8 nanoreactors by using silica as the template source (Figure 2b). The silica shells were first coated on the surface of Au NPs to form Au NP $@$ silica core–shell structures. Subsequent growth of ZIF-8 resulted in packaging multiple Au NP@silica to generate “raisin bun”– like composites. After etching the silica, multicavity hollow Au NPs@ZIF-8 nanostructures were acquired.[21] The hollow products were characteristic of both intrinsic monodispersed micropores and introduced macropores, and each hollow structure had only one Au nanoparticle inside.
Moreover, the exterior-template method is also suitable and available for fabricating periodic hollow MOF structures. A 2D hollow-structured MOF film was prepared based on a monolayer template assembled by PS spheres.[22] Through the color change of the MOF film, volatile organic compounds (VOCs) were visually and sensitively detected. Furthermore, a 3D hollow MOF structure with highly oriented and ordered macropores inside was constructed via a well-assembled opal template.[23] As displayed in Figure 2c, monodispersed PS spheres were assembled into the highly ordered opal structure. Afterward, the ZIF-8 precursor solution fully filled the interspace of the opal template. Then, it was immersed in a $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ and ${\mathrm{NH}}_{3}\cdot{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}$ mixture solution to realize a balance process between the growth of the MOF crystal and removal of the PS template. The prepared hollow MOF structures consisted of plenty of ordered holes, leading to better catalytic performance than conventional disordered macroporous ZIF-8 or polycrystal hollow ZIF-8.
## 2.1.2. Self-Template Method
The self-template method is an advanced template technique, which avoids extra removal of the exterior templates. Generally, the self-templates have two different types: 1) the intermediate products that spontaneously transform into hollow MOF structures, and 2) the reactants that act as sacrificial precursors for the formation of hollow MOFs via chemical reactions. Here, we only discuss the case of intermediate products as the template. Using the other one of the reactants as a template is introduced in the two-phase interface method section. The formation of the intermediate products involves a kinetic process, so it is hard to be predesigned. In previous work, we constructed triple-shelled $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ hollow microspheres using the self-template method.[24] Similar to hollow metal oxide microspheres, hollow MOFs could be synthesized by self-template method. Wang and co-workers presented hollow MOF-2 $(\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Ni})$ nanocubes with a hierarchical structure assembled by an MOF-2 $(\mathrm{Zn}/\mathrm{Ni})$ intermediate.[25] Hollow Zn–BTC microparticles were also synthesized with the similar process.[26]
An easy way to control the formation process of hollow MOF structures via the self-template method is surface protective modification of the intermediate products before a subsequent transformation reaction. Use of poly(vinylpyrrolidone) (PVP) has been reported as a surface protecting agent, followed by etching with HCl, to prepare hollow Ga-soc-MOF structures.[27] In addition, Caruso and co-workers found that phenolic acids could not only act as the surface protecting agents but also serve as unique etching agents during the self-template synthesis process, leading to a good balance between surface protection and template etching.[28] As a result, hollow-structured ZIF-8 crystals were built without destroying the parent crystalline frameworks, caused by the fact that the outer shell of the ZIF-8 was protected by the surface-attached acid and the inner part was etched by free $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ ions. Similarly, tannic acid was utilized to prepare hollow ZIF-8 structures through the same reaction process.[29]
In recent years, the etching method without a protecting agent has been developed. Huo and co-workers reported a multiple-step growth and etching process to prepare MOFs with hollow single-shelled, double-shelled, or even triple-shelled structures by using glacial acetic acid (Figure  3).[30] As shown in Figure 3, MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ crystals of truncated octahedral shape were synthesized by hydrothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}$ and 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}B D C)$ . After treatment with acetic acid solution, the inner crystallites were etched, resulting in hollow-structured MOF crystals. This synthetic strategy relies on the creation of inhomogeneous MOF crystals, in which the inner layer is chemically weaker than the outer layer and selectively etched by acetic acid. By repetition of MOF crystal growth and subsequent etching, double-shelled and triple-shelled hollow MIL-101(Cr) structures were acquired.
## 2.1.3. Two-Phase Interface Method
Different from the exterior-template and self-template methods, the two-phase interface method is more convenient and suitable for large-scale production. Generally, the synthesis of hollow MOF structures by the two-phase interface method includes three types: gas–liquid, liquid–liquid, and solid–liquid interface systems.
Among them, the gas–liquid interface system is widely applied, which involves two routes: 1) when the gas phase acts as the matrix, the liquid phase serves as the template (liquidin-gas), and 2) when the liquid phase functions as the matrix, the gas phase serves as the template (gas-in-liquid). An example of a liquid-in-gas system is shown in Figure  4, and the atomized droplets served as individual reactors to build and assemble hollow MOFs in air by using the spray-drying method. This synthetic approach successfully constructed 14 different types of hollow MOFs, including HKUST, Cu-BDC, NOTT-100 (NOTT: Nottingham), MIL-88A, MIL-88B, MOF14, MOF-74 (Zn, Ni, and Mg), UIO-66 (UIO: Universitetet i Oslo), ZIF-8, Cu-PB (PB: Prussian blue), MOF-5, and IRMOF-3 (IR: isoreticular). Also, MOFs could be encapsulated into the PS microspheres via the spray-drying method, which efficiently prevented MOF crystals from hydrolytic degradation.[31] Notably, the $\mathrm{MOF}@\mathrm{PS}$ structures demonstrated good stability against both liquid water and water vapors. On the other hand, a gas-in-liquid system for building hollow Zn–BTC polyhedra was implemented by adopting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ as a template.[32] $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was added to the ionic-liquid solution containing surfactants and Zn–BTC precursors, and the corresponding bubbles were generated in the continuous ionic-liquid phase under stirring. The Zn–BTC nanocrystals formed and assembled into hollow polyhedra at the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and ionic-liquid interface. Subsequently, different types of MOFs were designed with macroporous networks or hollow capsules by using the same approach.[33]
Additionally, the liquid–liquid interface can also be utilized to design and construct hollow MOF structures. In liquid–liquid interface synthesis, oil-in-water or water-in-oil emulsions are broadly adopted. Early in 2011, de Vos and co-workers indicated that Cu–BTC hollow structures could be synthesized through a liquid–liquid interface.[34] As shown in Figure  5b, the metal salt in an aqueous phase and the organic ligand in an organic solvent were stored in two separate syringe needles. Then, the aqueous droplets were added to the organic phase to generate the interface of two incompatible solutions, where the MOF was grown and the hollow HKUST-1 microstructures formed. Similarly, Xu and co-workers designed hollow-structured ZIF-8 by using this method,[35] and PVP was added to system to enhance the emulsion stability. The microfluidic channel is another efficient way to construct such an interface. Kim and co-workers reported functional bio-MOF hollow spheres by using microfluidic channels.[36] During the fabrication process, biologically active enzymes, such as formate dehydrogenase, horseradish peroxidase, and acetylcholinesterase, were successfully encapsulated. Interestingly, even double-shell hollow MOF structures might be built by microfluidic channels through forming aqueous (inner)–organic (middle)–aqueous (outer) double-shell emulsion droplets. In addition, hollow MOF structures with various shapes and compositions were prepared by the liquid–liquid-interface method. As shown in Figure 6, Eddaoudi and co-workers employed Fe-soc-MOF cubes as the building blocks to construct hollow superstructures via onestep assembly at the interface of two incompatible liquid phases,[37] and the relationship between the crystal nature and hollow structure was investigated. Furthermore, they assembled Ga-soc-MOF colloidosomes composed of highly uniform tetrakaidecahedron building blocks by using triethylamine as the structure-directing agents.[27] Bradshaw and co-workers reported successful preparation of hollow-structured MOF– polymer composite microcapsules via Pickering emulsions,[38] and the same approach was suitable for different MOFs such as
MIL-101, UIO-66, and ZIF-8. The prepared composite microcapsules demonstrated excellent interface characteristics, and, together with the small size pores inside, they were effective for the retention of encapsulated dye molecules. In the liquid– liquid-interface synthesis, a surfactant is usually used to stabilize the oil-in-water or water-in-oil emulsions. Interestingly enough, in single phase, even without the immiscible phase, the surfactant alone can promote the formation of hollow MOF structures at the interface. For example, Wang and co-workers developed a surface-energy–driven approach by using PVP to synthesize a series of well-defined hollow MOF (e.g., MOF-5, MOF-5(FeII), and MOF-5(FeIII)) nanocages.[10b] Wu and co-workers further followed Wang’s method to encapsulate phosphotungstic acid inside MOFs.[39] Lei and co-workers synthesized hollow ZIF-8 structures with the assistance of sodium dodecyl sulfate.[40]
The solid–liquid-interface method refers to solid-state reactants as templates for sequential conversion to hollowstructured products. One big advantage of this method is easy control over the products’ geometry. For instance, Lah and co-workers employed metal–organic polyhedra (MOP) single crystals as a sacrificial template to form hollow cuboctahedral Cu-MOFs.[41] Choe and co-workers further used MOPs to produce hollow MOFs with double shells.[42] As seen from Figure 7, MOP solid (UMOM-1) and 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]octane linker solution were mixed together. Since the cores did not make contact with the solution, only the external surfaces reacted to form MOP $@$ MOF core–shell structures (I-a). By a subsequent etching process (I-b′), hollow MOFs of a single shell were produced. In another method (I-b), epitaxial growth of an MOP precursor solution on the above core–shell structures produced $\mathsf{M O P}@\mathsf{M O F}@\mathsf{M O P}$ structures. Repeating process I-a, another MOF shell was formed. As a result, hollow MOFs with double shells were obtained after etching. Except for MOPs, MOFs have also been used as a sacrificial template to achieve another type of MOFs. As examples, ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 share the same crystal structure, and the only difference is the metal ions $(Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ for ZIF-8 and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ for ZIF-67). Wu and co-workers adopted ZIF-67 as the template and reacted with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ to synthesize hollow $Z{\mathrm{n}}/{\mathrm{Co}}$ mixed ZIFs.[43] This process was reported with more details by Fischer and co-workers.[44] Lu and co-workers employed MOF-5 nanocubes as the raw materials to produce hollow ZIF-8 spheres.[45] Interestingly, the template was cubes, but the product was spheres. This was because the cubes were etched into spheres by the organic imidazole ligands.
## 2.2. Hollow-MOF Derivatives
MOFs have some intrinsic drawbacks, like low electrical conductivity and poor stability, and these problems become more serious when MOFs are fabricated into hollow micro/ nanostructures, which limits their applications in energy conversion and storage processes. Therefore, hollow derivatives have been investigated by using MOFs as the precursors or sacrificial templates. Since MOFs are constructed from various metal ions and organic ligands through weak coordination bonds (Figure 1), many approaches are suitable to break these bonds within the frameworks to produce their derivatives, such as metal oxides, metal hydroxides, metal sulfides, metal phosphides, carbon matrixes, and others. Moreover, the derivatives may inherit the porous nature from the parent MOF, which endows them with significant advantages in organic catalysis, gas sensors, LIBs, supercapacitors, and electrocatalysis. Up to now, various physical and chemical methods have been utilized to prepare MOF derivatives, which are assigned to two main catalogs: 1) the pyrolysis process and 2) solid–liquid reaction. Especially, the pyrolysis process might be further classified into two subcatalogs based on different gas atmosphere conditions: 1) self-pyrolysis in an inert atmosphere $(\Nu_{2}$ , Ar, etc.), and 2) solid–gas reaction in reactive gases/vapors (air, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ , $\mathrm{PH}_{3}$ , etc.). On the other hand, the solid–liquid reaction upon MOFs with specific chemical reagents such as acids, alkalis, and metal salts is a rapid chemical process, including chemical etching, ion exchange, and so on. Moreover, these synthetic strategies are likely combined to prepare different derivatives. In the following discussion, we discuss different types of hollow MOF derivatives along with their synthetic approaches.
## 2.2.1. Derived Hollow Metal Hydroxides
MOF-derived hollow metal hydroxides are commonly produced by etching with chemical reagents in solution. Chemical reagents such as acids, bases, or metal salts have been used to break the coordination bonds between the metal ions and organic ligands, inducing the transformation to metal hydroxides.
As illustrated in Figure  8, ZIF-67(Co) nanocrystals were used as a sacrificial template to produce hollow structures of layered double hydroxides (LDHs) by etching in acidic solution. Through adding extra amounts of $\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2},$ $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}.$ and $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ solutions, respectively, many research groups have demonstrated the successful preparation of hollow $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Mg-Co}}$ LDH,[46] Ni–Co LDH,[46,47] and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}$ $\mathrm{LDH}^{\mathrm{[48]}}$ nanocages. Interestingly, by controlling the adding amount of acidic solution, one might obtain different hollow structures; for instance, addition of a small amount gave rise to the formation of a hollow structure with inner ZIF-67 and outer LDH, whereas adding an extra amount led to production of a hollow-structured LDH.
## 2.2.2. Derived Hollow Metal Oxides
Thanks to the abundant metal anodes (Fe, Co, Ni, Zn, Cu, Mg, Ti, Mn, and so on) inside, MOFs are ideal candidates for constructing various hollow metal oxides.[49] Pyrolysis of MOFs in air is an easy and rapid way to produce hollow metal oxides.
Hollow ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ was successfully derived from MIL-53(Fe) by the pyrolysis method.[50] As indicated in Figure  9a, MIL53(Fe) solid octahedra (concave) and yolk–shell octahedra were first synthesized by using microwave heating for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ and $^{6\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. Then, the MIL-53 octahedra with different structures were subjected to the pyrolysis process at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under air. Two derived ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ products of the same yolk–shell structure but different interior spaces were achieved. Other hollow metal oxides like $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ,[51] $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ ,[52] $\mathtt{C u O}$ ,[53] and $\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}[54]$ were also produced with ZIF-67, ZIF-8/MOF-5, HKUST-1, and Mn-BTC, respectively, as template materials by the pyrolysis method.
In addition to a single component, shells composed of multicomponents have also been fabricated. To obtain such hollow metal oxides, a pre-etching process needs to be adopted; for example, ZIF-67 is easily transformed to the metal hydroxides by in situ etching with ${\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{NO}}_{3})_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{Mg(NO_{3})_{2}}$ . As displayed in Figure 9b, ZIF-67 $@$ Ni–Co LDH core–shell particles were prepared by etching ZIF-67 with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ solution.[55] Subsequent pyrolysis of the obtained $Z_{ Ḋ }\mathrm{IF-}67@\mathrm{Ni-Co}$ LDHs at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under air resulted in the formation of hollow $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}@N i C o_{2}O_{4}}$ core–shell composites. This synthetic route can be applied for fabricating many hollow multicomponent metal oxides. When ZIF-8 was used as the template, hollow $\mathrm{ZnO@\mathbb{Q}n C o_{2}O_{4}}$ composites were synthesized.[56] Similarly, $\mathrm{ZnO@\overline{{\varrho}}M Z n O_{\boldsymbol{x}}^{\mathrm{\tiny{[57]}}}}$ and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{MCoO}_{x}^{\ [58]}$ $[\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Fe}$ , Co, Ni, Cu, and $\mathrm{Mg})$ ) core–shell structures were produced by the same synthetic route. Noteworthily, Lou and co-workers presented a new way to design and fabricate hollow-structured multicomponent metal oxides by performing thermal annealing of MOFs with polymetallic sources (isophthalic acid serving as an organic ligand) at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under air. A series of multicomponent metal oxides, such as Co–Ni, Mn–Co, Mn–Ni, Zn–Mn, and $\mathrm{\DeltaMn-Co-Ni,}$ , with hollow multishelled structures were synthesized by this simple and direct approach.[59]
## 2.2.3. Derived Hollow Carbon Matrixes
A hollow carbon matrix is the most popular MOF derivative. The abundant carbon from the organic ligands inside MOFs would be an ideal source for constructing a hollow carbon matrix, which serves as the support or active material. To fabricate a hollow carbon matrix, MOFs need to be subjected to self-pyrolysis under an inert atmosphere $(\Nu_{2}$ , Ar, etc.), which is different from the pyrolysis-involved synthesis of hollow metal oxides in air. Moreover, the obtained hollow carbon matrix likely retains the porous features inherited from the parent MOFs. Figure  10a illustrates the preparation procedure of a typical hollow carbon matrix. ZIF-8 nanocrystals were utilized as the sacrificial template, and their inner parts of low stability were first etched by a tannic acid solution. Afterward, the as-formed hollow ZIF-8 was pyrolyzed at $600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, followed by a second etching of the remaining $Z\mathrm{n}$ with HCl solution, resulting in the production of a hollow carbon matrix.[29]
When the hollow carbon matrix serves as the support material or the protective shell, the second etching process is no longer needed after the pyrolysis treatment. As shown in Figure 10b, porous $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}@C}$ octahedra with hollow interiors were prepared by using $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -modified MOF-5 octahedra as self-sacrificial templates.[60] The hollow-structured $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -modified MOF-5 octahedra were prepared by mixing ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{3}$ , $\mathrm{\Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}$ , terephthalic acid, and PVP in a DMF/ethanol solvent. After pyrolysis at $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, hollow structures assembled by many subunits of $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}@C}$ were formed. Significantly, the carbon shell on the outer layer of the $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}}$ not only served as good transport channels, but also improved the stability of the hollow structures during the catalytic reactions.
Also, a hollow carbon matrix containing metal NPs or metal single-atomic sites was also prepared without the removal of the metal species. Co-DABCO-TPA (DABCO: 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]­ octane; TPA: terephthalic acid) MOFs were employed as templates upon pyrolysis treatment at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under an Ar atmosphere, which reduced ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions within the MOFs to Co NPs or Co single-atomic sites.[61] This is an easy and rapid way to design highly efficient catalysts.
## 2.2.4. Hollow Metal Phosphides, Metal Sulfides, and Metal Selenides
Other derivatives, such as hollow metal phosphides, metal sulfides, and metal selenides, can be acquired with MOF templates through phosphorization, sulfurization, and selenylation processes, respectively. Lou and co-workers used ZIF-67 nanocrystals as templates and mixed them with $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ solution, giving rise to the formation of hollow ZIF-6 $7@C o-\mathrm{Ni}$ LDHs core–shell nanoboxes (Figure 11).[47] Next, the ZIF-67 $@$ Co-Ni LDH nanoboxes together with ${\mathrm{NaH}}_{2}{\mathrm{PO}}_{2}$ (a phosphorus source) were transferred to a tubular furnace with pyrolysis treatment at $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Finally, Ni–Co mixed hollow metal phosphides (NiCoP/C) were obtained with coating carbon shells, which were confirmed by electron microscopy imaging (Figure 11). It should be noted that there is a difference in the preparation process between hollow metal sulfides and metal phosphides. During the etching process of ZIF-67, sulfide ions were added to the reaction solution for modifying the LDH surface, resulting in sulfur-rich products. Thus, no additional sulfur source is needed to be added during the following pyrolysis treatment. With respect to the synthesis of hollow metal selenides, Co-based MOFs and selenium powders were directly mixed together during the pyrolysis process to implement selenylation of metals at $550~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under an Ar atmosphere.[62]
## 2.2.5. Other Derivatives
The weak coordination bonds inside an MOF determine the metastable nature, in other words, they are excellent templates for the fabrication of many hollow structures via physical/chemical removal under mild conditions.[63] For example, UiO-66 particles were utilized as the templates and coated with different microporous organic networks (MON-1, MON-2, MON-3, and MON-4). After being etched by HF solution, hollow-structured H-MON-1, H-MON-2, H-MON-3, and H-MON-4 were successfully prepared.[64]
## 3. Applications
Both hollow MOFs and their derivatives possess unique merits, such as abundant metal active sites, porous structures, and internal spacing, which endow them with promising applications in organic catalysis, gas sensors, LIBs, supercapacitors, electrocatalysis, and so on.
## 3.1. Organic Catalysis
Organic catalysis is one of the central topics in chemistry because of its wide applications in medicine, materials, agriculture, and the environment. When hollow-structured MOFs and their derivatives are utilized as capsule reactors for catalysis, they have demonstrated remarkable performance in selective hydrogenation, organic degradation, goal-directed oxidation, and so on.
Hybridizing MOFs with noble-metal NPs is an efficient way to design composite catalysts with high selectivity and stability, because the MOFs not only determine the selectivity through their pore windows but also prevent the embedded NPs from aggregating during the catalytic process. In our previous work, catalysts characteristic of Pt NPs embedded between the core and the shell of MIL-101 $(\mathrm{MIL-101}(\mathrm{Cr})@\mathrm{Pt@MIL-101}(\mathrm{Fe}))$ were explored and found to possess high selectivity and excellent stability in the conversion of $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated aldehydes.[4b] Similarly, hollow MOFs composited with noble-metal NPs demonstrated significant selectivity in hydrogenation reactions. For example, Xu and co-workers fabricated a series of catalysts by encapsulation of Pd nanocubes by hollow ZIF-8 of different thicknesses. When the $\mathrm{Pd}\ @\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8$ catalysts were used in the liquid-phase hydrogenation of 1-hexene, trans-stilbene, or tetraphenylethylene, the reactants with bigger sizes exhibited the lowest conversion.[35] As shown in Figure 12, 1-hexene, with the smallest size among the three reactants, presented the highest conversion. In addition, the influence of the shell thickness on the hydrogenation performance was investigated. Evidently, the Pd@ZIF-8 catalysts with thicker shell thickness possessed lower conversion efficiency but higher selectivity (Figure 12). The increased selectivity of the hydrogenation reaction was attributed to special pore windows inside the ZIF-8 shells. A similar result was reported by using void $@$ HKUST-1/Pd@ZIF-8 hollow catalysts in the hydrogenation reaction.[20]
Hollow MOF structures can also act as nanoreactors. Wang and co-workers synthesized PdCu@MOF-5(FeIII) yolk–shell structures and employed them in hydrogenation.[10b] They found that such hollow structures had better activity than PdCu dendrites, because the hydrogenation reaction was accelerated in the hollow nanoreactors. Analogously, hollow phosphotungstic acid (PTA) $@$ CdCu@MOF-5(FeIII) yolk–shell nanostructures showed good selective hydrogenation performance toward palmitic acid.[39] Except for the activity, the MOFs nanoreactors exhibit a considerable effect on the catalytic selectivity. Hollow $\mathrm{Pd}@Z\mathrm{n-Co}$ ZIF catalysts displayed higher conversion and better selectivity in semihydrogenation of the acetylene reaction compared with solid-structured samples, since the hollow structures enhanced the diffusion process and provided rich accessible active sites.[43]
Hollow MOF derivatives have shown good performance in organic catalysis. For example, ZIF-8 or ZIF-67 nanocrystals were first loaded with Pt nanoparticles, and were then were covered with tannic acid or resorcinol–formaldehyde (RF) polymer, followed by ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ doping. After the pyrolysis process, a hollow catalyst, with the structure of bimetallic NPs encapsulated by hollow porous carbon $({\mathrm{PtCo}}@{\mathrm{HPC}})$ , was obtained.[65] The $\operatorname{PtCo}\ @\operatorname{HPC}$ catalyst presented high conversion in the hydrogenation of nitroarenes and even better selectivity compared with commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalysts. Hollow MOF derivatives were also applied in catalytic selective oxidation. Li et  al. found that the Fe–Co/C catalysts derived from MIL-45b afforded near $100\%$ yield of 2,5-diformylfuran produced from 5-hydroxymethylfurfural.[66] Moreover, MOF-derived metal oxides have tunable bandgaps, and are very suitable for photocatalysis. Li and co-workers reported $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{ZnO@\mathcal{Q}-N-C o}}$ core–shell structures derived from hollow $Z{\mathrm{n}}/{\mathrm{Co}}$ -based-ZIF, which exhibited good activity in the photocatalytic degradation of methyl orange.[67] The hollow-structured $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ spheres were coated with MIL101 shells, which demonstrated good performance in photocatalysis for the degradation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ .[68]
We name the other catalysts, which are not included in abovementioned types, as complex catalysts. Zn/Ni-MOF-2 nanosheets were assembled into hollow nanocube structures, where Pd clusters were immobilized inside. These $\operatorname{Pd}\ @Z\mathrm{n}/$ Ni-MOF-2 core–shell catalysts exhibited good performance in the alkoxycarbonylation reaction of aryl iodides.[25] Li and co-workers reported that ZIF-8 single-crystals with ordered macropores had excellent catalytic activity in the Knoevenagel reaction between benzaldehydes and malono-nitriles,[23] which was better than conventional bulk ZIF-8 or macroporous ZIF-8 synthesized by using disordered PSs as templates. This result suggests an important effect of the highly ordered pore structure on the heterogeneous catalytic performance; namely, it makes the reactants make efficient contact with the catalysts and thus promotes the reaction.
## 3.2. Gas Sensors
Metal oxides have been recognized as promising gas-sensor materials because of their tunable energy band structure and good electron-transmission property. Hence, MOFs have been utilized to fabricate hollow-structured metal oxides for application in gas sensors. Hollow derivatives are characteristic of hierarchical porous channels and have high specific surface area for promoting gas diffusion and adsorption. Such features, combined with the appropriate bandgap, would increase the response value and decrease the response-recovery time. Wu and co-workers synthesized hollow $z\mathrm{nO}$ nanocages by pyrolysis of MOF-5. When used as sensors, the hollow $\mathrm{{}}Z\mathrm{{nO}}$ nanocages showed sub-ppm level sensitivity with $2.3\mathrm{ppm}^{-1}$ toward 0.1 ppm benzene, as well as ppb level sensitivity with $15.3~\mathrm{ppm^{-1}}$ toward 50 ppb acetone.[52] Furthermore, the MOFderived hollow $\mathrm{ZnO@\@ZnCo_{2}O_{4}}$ nanocages were used to detect xylene,[56] while $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnCo_{2}O_{4}},$ [69] $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}}^{\mathrm{/70]}}$ and $\mathrm{PdO}/$ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}[71]$ hollow nanocages exhibited good response to acetone.
MOF-based membranes have been extensively studied for gas or liquid separation. Actually, such membranes may have the intriguing applications in sensors. Li et  al. found that ordered hollow MOF-based membranes have a distinct color output toward $\mathrm{VOCs}^{[22]}$ and are promising for visual gas detection.
## 3.3. Batteries
MOF-derived hollow structures consisting of or doped with metal oxides $(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ ,[72] $\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ ,[54] $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ ,[50,73] $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ,[51,74] $\mathtt{C u O}_{\mathtt{i n}}$ ,[53,75] $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ ,[57,60,76] and so on) and metal sulfides $(\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8},\mathrm{\ell^{77]}\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{S},\mathrm{\ell^{78]}}}$ and so on) have been extensively used as anode electrode materials in LIBs with large capacity, high power, and long cycle stability. It is believed that hollow porous electrode materials containing wellconnected carbon matrices and metal-based compounds may not only support abundant active sites and fast ion transport, but also help buffer the volume expansion caused by $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ intercalation. For example, ZIF-67 templates were utilized to produce $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ embedded carbon nanocages.[77] Such $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@\$ carbon nanocages demonstrated a large energy density of 707 W $\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . Good reversible capabilities of 536, 489, 438, 393, 345, and $278\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ were also obtained at 0.2, 0.5, 1, 2, 5, and 10C, respectively. Moreover, such electrode materials exhibited good cycling performance with $74\%$ capacitance retention after 150 cycles at 1C, supporting the view of good performance with hollow $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}@$ carbon nano­ cages in LIBs. Benefiting from the rapid ion transport and good electron conductivity within 3D interconnected carbon matrices, $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}}\textcircled{\Delta}$ carbon nanostructures produced by MOF-5 nanocrystals[60] displayed an outstanding electrochemical lithiumstorage performance as anode materials with large capacity, high power density, and long cycling property in LIBs. Double-shelled hollow $\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}@\mathrm{LDH}$ nanocages were synthesized and utilized as the sulfur host in Li–S batteries (Figure 13).[79] This composite cathode of $\mathrm{Co(OH)}_{2}@\mathrm{LDH/S}$ possesses multiple advantages in that the multishelled hollow structures within the cathode would not only increase the encapsulating amount of sulfur, but also provide a large surface area for chemically bonding with polysulfides. As a result, it showed excellent cycling performance at both 0.1 and 0.5C after 100 cycles and high-rate capacity with large sulfur loading of $3\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
## 3.4. Supercapacitors
Supercapacitors are another application that is widely investigated with regard to MOF-derived hollow structures. Until now, hollow-structured metal oxides $(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [80] $\mathrm{MnO}_{2}$ ,[81] $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ,[82] NiO,[59,81,83] $Z\mathrm{nO}^{[83]})$ and metal sulfides $(\mathrm{Ni_{3}S_{2},^{[84]}C o_{9}S_{8},^{[15,85]}Z n S^{[86]}})$ have been designed and utilized as anode materials in supercapacitors. Porous hollow structures have demonstrated significant properties in supercapacitor applications because they provide a rich redox chemistry with abundant active sites, rapid ion-transport channels, and good electrochemical stability. For instance, sandwich hollow $\mathrm{C/NiCo-LDH/Co_{9}S_{8}}$ structures were utilized as anode materials in supercapacitors, resulting in a large specific capacitance of $1653\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at $4\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . In addition, excellent cycling stability with $93.2\%$ retention capacitance after 3000 cycles was exhibited.[85] Lee et  al. reported hollow $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocages with outstanding performance in supercapacitors.[87] When tested with $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanocages at an optimal $\mathrm{\DeltaNi/Co}$ ratio value of 1, the supercapacitors exhibited a large specific capacitance of $1931\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and a capacitance retention of $69.5\%$ after 5000 cycles. Analogously, the composite electrode of $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}/$ graphene 3D hydrogel supercapacitors displayed a higher specific capacitance of $2870.8\mathrm{~F~g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ and a significant improvement in the cyclic stability with $81\%$ capacitance retention after 5000 cycles compared with pure $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Co}_{3-x}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ electrode.[59]
## 3.5. Electrocatalysis
Electrocatalysis is an important process in energy conversion. Large surface area and abundant accessible active sites also enable MOF-derived hollow structures significant advantages in the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR),[55,88] oxygen evolution reaction (OER),[47,58a,62,89] hydrogen evolution reaction (HER),[89b,c,90] and so on.[91] Wang and co-workers reported a bifunctional oxygen electrocatalyst, namely, ZIF-67-derived hollow structures containing N-doped carbon nanotubes and Co NPs encapsulated by few-layered carbon shells.[92] When used in the ORR, this electrocatalyst exhibited superior ORR activity in $0.5~\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution compared with commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ , as well as high ORR activity in $0.5\mathrm{~M~}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ solution with an onset potential of $0.85\mathrm{V}.$ Moreover, it displayed a better OER performance of $93~\mathrm{mV}$ decade−1 from the Tafel slope than that of $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ ( $118\mathrm{mV}$ decade−1).
## 4. Summary and Outlook
We have summarized the synthetic methods and practical applications of hollow MOF and derivatives. The synthetic methods of hollow MOF nanostructures are classified by how the templates are built, and each method has its own advantages and disadvantages. The exterior-template method is direct and universal, and, theoretically, it can be utilized for the fabrication of any hollow MOF structures if one is able to control the MOF’s growth and template removal. However, its multiplestep operation and template waste production will seriously impede the broad application of the exterior-template method. Alternatively, the self-template method adopts the intermediate products as the templates during the fabrication process, which is a rapid and cost-effective approach. The self-template method is limited to the design and formation of the intermediates. Up to now, the two-phase interface method is the most widely used approach, by which the formation of hollow MOF shells occurs at the interface of two phases. Additionally, it deserves to be pointed out that the two-phase interface method is suitable for large-scale production of hollow MOFs, but it is urged that the precise regulation of the hollow structures, including size and shape, is improved.
Hollow MOF derivatives are promising candidates for applications in energy conversion and storage owing to their hierarchical porous structures, rich accessible active sites, high chemical stability, and good electron conductivity. The pyrolysis process and solid–liquid reaction are the most used ways for constructing hollow MOF derivatives. Generally, these two approaches are suitable for the preparation of various hollow MOF derivatives, such as metal oxides, carbon matrixes, metal hydroxides, metal phosphides, metal sulfides, and metal selenides. However, the periodic pore structure of the pristine
MOFs is usually destroyed by using these two methods, thus sacrificing the MOF-based specificity in practical applications. Therefore, elaborate control over the experiments, for instance, programmed pyrolysis or solid–liquid reaction, needs to be explored. Furthermore, it is of great interest to create new types of hollow MOF derivatives, such as MXenes and perovskites, by using MOFs as precursors.
The development of hollow MOFs and their derivatives is still at the beginning stage. Their synthetic methods and practical applications must be expanded and improved in future research. Therefore, more efforts should be focused on the precise synthesis and large-scale production of hollow MOFs and derivatives (Figure  14). A composite structure will be highly desirable for both hollow MOFs and hollow-MOF derivatives. For instance, constructing a stable additional shell over the less stable pristine hollow structures will significantly improve their stability. Hollow composite catalysts are expected to efficiently improve not only activity but also selectivity toward high-value added products. Additionally, loading functional materials, such as NPs, clusters, single atomic sites, etc., is also an economic, rapid, and convenient method to regulate the optical, electrical, or catalytic properties of hollow MOFs or their derivatives.
Controlling the geometric structures of hollow MOFs or their derivatives through precise synthesis is a very important and efficient way to design and fabricate functional materials (Figure 14). In addition to the usual spherical hollow structures, preparing new shapes of hollow MOFs or their derivatives, such as cube-, cylinder-, octahedron-, sheet-, or seaurchin-like ones, will greatly enrich the warehouse of hollow materials, which eventually affects their physiochemical properties like stability, activity, selectivity, mass transfer effect, and so on. Particularly, one should take advantage of the ordered pore arrangement inside the walls of the hollow structures to promote the application performance in drug delivery, chemical sensors/biosensors, organic catalysis, and so on. Recently, ultrathin Co- and Ni-based MOF nanosheets with no need of carbonization were found to possess excellent activity and stability in the OER.[49] This may give us confidence in designing 2D hollow MOFs through tuning the shell thickness for electrochemical applications. Also, the tunable pore-size windows and the adjustable inner space within hollow MOFs will be expected to promote their properties in broad practical applications.
Last but not least, the large-scale production of hollow MOFs or derivatives is important for real applications or industry production. New synthetic approaches, such as spray drying, electrospinning, and hydrothermal methods, are called for to realize precise control as well as mass production of hollow MOFs and their derivatives. Altogether, the study of hollow MOFs and hollow-MOF derivatives is at a burgeoning stage, and there are many opportunities waiting to be explored.
